      "lem:response-fiber-compatibility-derived"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(M=2K|\\mathcal X||\\mathcal Z|\\) and let \\(E_n\\) be the event that every true cell probability belongs to its interval.  Conditional on all row totals \\((N_{xz})_{x,z}\\), each marginal cell count \\(N_{xzay}\\) is binomial with total \\(N_{xz}\\) and success probability \\(p_{xzay}\\).  By \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}clopper\\text{-}pearson\\text{-}cell\\text{-}coverage}\\), its conditional noncoverage probability is at most \\(\\alpha/M\\); when \\(N_{xz}=0\\), it is zero by the \\([0,1]\\) convention.  Dependence among cells is immaterial to the union bound, so\n\\[\n \\Pr(E_n^c\\mid(N_{xz})_{x,z})\n \\leq\\sum_{x,z,a,y}\\frac{\\alpha}{M}=\\alpha.\n\\]\nTaking expectations gives \\(\\Pr(E_n)\\geq1-\\alpha\\), for every sample size and every law in \\(\\mathfrak P_{\\mathrm{IV}}\\).\n\nOn \\(E_n\\), the true table has all interval memberships and multinomial row-sum constraints required by \\(\\mathcal C_n(\\alpha)\\).  Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}response\\mbox{-}fiber\\mbox{-}compatibility\\mbox{-}derived}\\) supplies \\(\\mathcal W_x(p_x)\\neq\\varnothing\\), so \\(p\\in\\mathcal C_n(\\alpha)\\).  The definition of the optimizer confidence set then yields, simultaneously for every \\(x\\),\n\\[\n Q_x^*(p_x)\\subseteq\\bigcup_{p'\\in\\mathcal C_n(\\alpha)}Q_x^*(p'_x)\n =\\widehat{\\mathcal Q}_{x,n}(\\alpha).\n\\]\nCombining this implication with \\(\\Pr(E_n)\\geq1-\\alpha\\) proves the advertised simultaneous coverage.  Since the lower bound does not depend on the particular law, it is uniform over \\(\\mathfrak P_{\\mathrm{IV}}\\).\n\nFor computation, the response roster is finite.  On each roster, primal feasibility, dual feasibility, complementarity, and equality of the primal and dual values are polynomial equalities and weak inequalities in \\((p',q,t,\\lambda)\\), because every entry of \\(G_x(p'_x)\\) is affine in \\(p'_x\\).  Enumerating all primal--dual active bases therefore enumerates the complete semialgebraic KKT graph.  At a tied law, the full optimizer face is the convex hull of its optimal primal basic solutions, so retaining the face, rather than selecting one basic optimizer, includes every mixture on an intersection.  The interval convention at a zero row imposes no missing constraint, and weak KKT inequalities retain boundary and zero-count cases.  Projection of this finite graph over \\(p'\\in\\mathcal C_n(\\alpha)\\) is exactly the union in the definition.",
